MTM.propertyFaq = [
 ["Can foreigners buy property in Malaysia?","Yes, foreigners can purchase property in Malaysia subject to minimum price thresholds that vary by state. In Johor, the minimum is typically RM 1 million for landed property and RM 500,000 for condominiums in designated zones. State consent may be required for certain purchases."],
 ["What is the typical rental deposit in Johor?","The standard rental deposit structure is 2+1: two months' security deposit plus half a month's utility deposit. Some landlords may request 3+1 for higher-end properties. The security deposit is refundable at the end of your tenancy, minus any deductions for damages."],
 ["How long does the property buying process take?","The typical timeline is 3-6 months from offer to key collection. This includes property search (2-8 weeks), loan approval (2-4 weeks), SPA signing (14 days after booking), balance payment (3 months from SPA), and title transfer (1-3 months)."],
 ["What is stamp duty and how much is it?","Stamp duty is a tax on property transactions. For purchases up to RM 100,000, it's 1%. From RM 100,001 to RM 500,000, it's 2%. From RM 500,001 to RM 1,000,000, it's 3%. Above RM 1,000,000, it's 4%. First-time homebuyers may be eligible for exemptions."],
 ["Do I need a lawyer to buy property?","Yes, it's strongly recommended and essentially required. A conveyancing lawyer handles the Sale and Purchase Agreement (SPA), title searches, loan documentation, and ensures the transaction is legally sound. Legal fees typically range from 0.4-1% of the purchase price."],
 ["What's the difference between freehold and leasehold?","Freehold means you own the land indefinitely. Leasehold means you have rights to the land for a fixed period (typically 99 years), after which it reverts to the state unless renewed. Freehold properties generally command higher prices but leasehold can offer good value."],
 ["Can I rent out my property if I'm a foreigner?","Yes, foreign property owners can rent out their properties in Malaysia. Rental income is subject to Malaysian income tax at graduated rates (up to 30% for non-residents). You'll need to file annual tax returns and may benefit from engaging a local property manager."],
 ["What is the MM2H programme and how does it affect property purchase?","Malaysia My Second Home (MM2H) is a long-term visa programme for foreigners. MM2H visa holders may have different minimum purchase thresholds depending on the state and tier of their visa. It also provides benefits like easier banking and longer-term residency."],
 ["Are there any areas foreigners cannot buy?","Yes, certain restrictions apply. Malay Reserved Land cannot be purchased by non-Malays. Agricultural land and properties below minimum thresholds are also restricted. Some states have additional requirements like state consent. Always verify with a lawyer before committing."],
 ["What ongoing costs should I budget for as a property owner?","Monthly costs include: maintenance fees (RM 200-800 for condos), quit rent (annual land tax, RM 50-500), assessment rates (local council tax, twice yearly), insurance, and repairs. For landed properties, factor in gardening, pool maintenance if applicable, and general upkeep."]
];

