MTM.commuteFaq = [
 ["What is the VEP (Vehicle Entry Permit) for Singapore?","The VEP is a mandatory permit for all foreign-registered vehicles entering Singapore. As of 2024, the fee ranges from SGD 20-35 depending on the day and time of entry. Peak hours (weekday mornings 7-9am, evenings 5-8pm) attract higher charges. You must register your vehicle and apply for VEP through the Land Transport Authority (LTA) of Singapore."],
 ["Which crossing is better - Second Link or Woodlands?","It depends on your location and destination. Second Link (Tuas) is ideal for those living in western Johor (Iskandar Puteri, Gelang Patah) heading to western Singapore. Woodlands Causeway is better for those in eastern Johor (JB City, Taman Molek) going to central/north Singapore. Second Link generally has shorter queues but longer distance for central Singapore destinations."],
 ["What are the peak hours to avoid?","The busiest times are weekday mornings (6:30-9:30am) for Malaysia→Singapore, and evenings (5:30-9pm) for Singapore→Malaysia. Friday evenings and Sunday evenings are particularly congested. The least busy times are typically mid-morning (10am-12pm) and mid-afternoon (2-4pm) on weekdays."],
 ["Can I use Touch n Go or EZ-Link at the crossings?","Touch n Go is accepted at Malaysian toll plazas. For Singapore, you'll need an In-Vehicle Unit (IU) with a stored-value card (CashCard or EZ-Link with auto-top-up). Many Malaysians install a Singapore IU in their car specifically for regular crossings. The AutoPass Card is another option that works at both crossings."],
 ["What documents do I need for daily commuting?","You'll need: valid passport, valid vehicle registration documents, road tax, insurance (valid for both countries), VEP sticker on your vehicle, and an IU unit for Singapore tolls. For work commuters, ensure your employment pass/work permit allows multiple entries. Keep digital copies accessible on your phone as backup."],
 ["Is carpooling or ride-sharing available?","Yes, there are several carpooling and cross-border transport options. Private car-sharing groups exist on Facebook and WhatsApp. Services like Grab offer cross-border rides (at higher rates). Some employers arrange shuttle buses for employees. For regular commuters, joining a carpool can significantly reduce costs and allow use of carpool lanes during peak hours."],
 ["How will the RTS Link change commuting?","The Johor Bahru-Singapore Rapid Transit System (RTS) Link, opening in February 2027, will connect Bukit Chagar (JB) to Woodlands North (SG) in just 5 minutes. This will dramatically reduce commute times and costs for those living near JB city. It's expected to handle 10,000 passengers per hour in each direction."],
 ["What if I miss my Touch n Go or IU payment?","If you pass through without sufficient balance, the system will record the transaction. For Touch n Go, you'll need to top up and pay at a Touch n Go hub. For Singapore IU, LTA will send a notice to your registered address with the outstanding amount plus an admin fee. Repeated violations can result in fines or entry restrictions."]
];

