MTM.colFaq = [
 ["Is the cost of living in Johor really that much lower than Singapore?","Yes. On average, everyday expenses in Johor Bahru are 40–60% lower than Singapore. But the bigger story is what that savings unlocks: larger homes, private pools, domestic help, golf memberships, and international schooling all become attainable at a fraction of the price. Many families find they can upgrade their entire lifestyle rather than simply spend less."],
 ["What quality of life can I expect for RM 5,000–8,000 per month?","A monthly budget of RM 5,000–8,000 comfortably covers a mid-range condo, groceries, dining out several times a week, a gym membership, reliable internet, and a car. It's a lifestyle that would cost two to three times more in Singapore or Kuala Lumpur, and considerably more in most Western cities."],
 ["Are there hidden costs expats often overlook?","A few items catch newcomers off guard: annual car insurance and road tax, condo maintenance fees (typically RM 200–600/month depending on facilities), and the cost of alcohol which is taxed heavily in Malaysia. On the flip side, expats are often pleasantly surprised by how affordable services like cleaning, gardening, and private healthcare are."],
 ["How does healthcare cost compare, and is the quality reliable?","Private healthcare in Johor is world-class (several hospitals hold JCI accreditation) and a specialist consultation typically costs RM 80–150 compared to SGD 100–200+ in Singapore. Many expats find they can afford comprehensive private health insurance and still spend less overall than they did on co-pays and premiums abroad."],
 ["Is it worth hiring domestic help in Johor?","Absolutely. Part-time house cleaning runs RM 50–150 per session, and many families hire weekly cleaners, gardeners, and pool maintenance for a combined monthly cost that wouldn't cover a single cleaning session in London or Sydney. It frees up time and genuinely elevates day-to-day living."],
 ["How do international school fees compare?","International schools in Johor range from RM 15,000 to RM 50,000 per year, roughly a third to a half of equivalent schools in Singapore or Hong Kong. Several follow the British, IB, or American curricula with strong academic outcomes, making quality education accessible without the financial strain."],
 ["Should I worry about the MYR exchange rate weakening?","Currency fluctuations are normal, but for those earning in SGD, USD, or EUR, a weaker ringgit actually increases your purchasing power in Malaysia. Many cross-border families keep savings in their home currency and convert as needed to optimise value."],
 ["What's the best way to transfer money to Malaysia?","Digital remittance services like Wise, Instarem, and YouTrip consistently offer rates much closer to the mid-market rate than traditional banks. The rate comparison in the Exchange tab gives you a quick side-by-side. Setting up a Malaysian bank account (e.g., CIMB or Maybank) also simplifies recurring payments like rent and utilities."]
];

