MTM.sezFaq = [
 ["What exactly is the JS-SEZ?","The Johor-Singapore Special Economic Zone is a bilateral economic initiative between Malaysia and Singapore to create a seamless cross-border economic zone. It aims to combine Johor's land, labour, and cost advantages with Singapore's capital, technology, and global connectivity."],
 ["How is the JS-SEZ different from Iskandar Malaysia?","Iskandar Malaysia is a Malaysian-only economic corridor established in 2006. The JS-SEZ builds on Iskandar's infrastructure but adds a bilateral dimension with Singapore, including coordinated tax incentives, customs facilitation, and cross-border workforce arrangements."],
 ["Can I set up a company in the JS-SEZ as a foreigner?","Yes. Many sectors allow 100% foreign equity. The JS-SEZ is specifically designed to attract foreign investment with streamlined registration, tax incentives, and fast-track work permits. Certain sectors may have additional licensing requirements."],
 ["What visa do I need to work in the JS-SEZ?","Employees typically need an Employment Pass (EP) sponsored by their employer. Business owners may apply for an EP or use an MM2H/PVIP visa if they meet the criteria. The JS-SEZ may introduce special visa categories as implementation progresses."],
 ["When will the JS-SEZ be fully operational?","The framework was signed in 2024, with implementation details being finalised throughout 2025. Many incentives in Iskandar Malaysia already apply. Full JS-SEZ-specific incentives and governance are expected to roll out progressively from 2025–2027."],
 ["Is Forest City part of the JS-SEZ?","Forest City sits within the Iskandar Malaysia region and has been designated as a special financial zone with duty-free status. It is expected to play a significant role in the JS-SEZ, particularly for financial services and family offices."],
 ["How will the RTS Link affect the JS-SEZ?","The Rapid Transit System (RTS) Link, opening in February 2027, will connect JB Sentral to Singapore's Woodlands North MRT station in about 5 minutes. This dramatically reduces cross-border commute friction and is a key enabler for the JS-SEZ workforce model."]
];
